WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.807
Python type hints are useful developer guidance.

2
00:00:02.807 --> 00:00:09.223
They make code easier to read, improve editor support, and allow static
analysis tools to catch certain mistakes.

3
00:00:09.223 --> 00:00:13.033
But type hints usually do not enforce runtime safety by themselves.

4
00:00:13.033 --> 00:00:21.922
If a function is annotated to receive an integer, Python will not
automatically reject a string unless the code or framework performs an
actual runtime check.

5
00:00:21.922 --> 00:00:29.007
Dataclasses, TypedDict, attrs, and similar patterns can improve structure,
but they have different enforcement behavior.

6
00:00:29.007 --> 00:00:34.755
A dataclass can organize fields, but it does not automatically validate
every field's business rule.

7
00:00:34.755 --> 00:00:43.176
TypedDict helps describe dictionary shapes to type checkers, but raw JSON
from a request still needs runtime validation before it should be trusted.

8
00:00:43.176 --> 00:00:47.520
These tools support clarity; they do not remove the need for boundary
checks.

9
00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:52.600
Pydantic is commonly used for runtime validation and data models in Python
applications.

10
00:00:52.600 --> 00:00:59.551
It can parse inputs, enforce field types, apply constraints, validate
nested structures, and produce structured errors.

11
00:00:59.551 --> 00:01:04.229
Frameworks such as FastAPI can integrate model validation into request
handling.

12
00:01:04.229 --> 00:01:12.383
Even then, developers should understand what is being coerced, what is
being rejected, and which business rules still require explicit checks.

13
00:01:12.383 --> 00:01:15.859
Runtime checks for external input are the security boundary.

14
00:01:15.859 --> 00:01:23.879
Static types, annotations, and model definitions are helpful only when the
data crossing into trusted code is actually parsed and validated.

15
00:01:23.879 --> 00:01:34.840
A mature approach uses type hints for developer clarity, schema or model
validation for input boundaries, and explicit business-rule checks where
correctness depends on application context.
